---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { meta } from '../lib/data';
import { site } from '../../site.config';
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="What this site is, how it is produced, and what that means for how much you should trust it."
  path="/about"
>
  <div class="wrap narrow prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'About' }]} />
    <h1>About {site.name}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      {site.name} is an automatically generated index of {meta.counts.software} self-hosted,
      open-source projects, scored on how actively each one is maintained.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is made</h2>
    <p>
      This is worth stating plainly, because it changes how you should read the site: every page
      here is generated by a script. A scheduled job pulls the latest public repository metadata,
      recomputes every score, and rebuilds all {Object.values(meta.counts).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)}
      {' '}pages from scratch. Nobody writes these pages by hand, and nobody edits an individual
      score.
    </p>
    <p>
      That has a clear cost and a clear benefit. The cost: there is no hands-on reviewer who has
      personally deployed each of these projects, so you will not find installation war stories or
      opinionated feature critiques here. The benefit: nothing rots. A hand-written "best of"
      article is wrong within a year and nobody goes back to fix it. Every number on this site is
      recomputed weekly, and a project that stops shipping loses its rank automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is good for</h2>
    <p>
      Ruling options out. If you are choosing between six projects that all look plausible, the
      health score tells you which two have not had a commit in a year — the ones that will cost
      you a migration in eighteen months. Narrow the field here, then evaluate features yourself
      on the survivors.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is not good for</h2>
    <p>
      Final decisions. The score does not know whether a project has the specific feature you
      need, whether its security model suits you, or whether you will enjoy running it. It also
      systematically understates small, finished tools that genuinely need no further commits —
      a limitation documented honestly on the <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How it pays for itself</h2>
    <p>
      Through hosting affiliate links, disclosed on every page that carries them and listed on the
      <a href="/disclosure">disclosure page</a>. Software projects cannot pay to be listed or
      ranked, and the ranking is produced by a script that has no input they could buy.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>
